## Why This Is Asked
This question tests understanding of per-tenant data isolation, retention controls, and safe data purging in a live streaming system. It requires concrete API shape, data model decisions, and a practical testing strategy suitable for beginners while exposing real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped retention policy and TTL
- Message metadata and tombstone semantics for replay
- Purge safety relative to consumer offsets
- Canary-based testing and end-to-end validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: set TTL for a tenant-topic
async function setTTL(tenantId, topic, ttlMs) {
  // persist TTL in config store per tenant-topic
  await configStore.set(`/tenants/${tenantId}/topics/${topic}/ttl`, ttlMs);
}

// Pseudo: purge loop (simplified)
function purgeOldMessages(tenantId, topic) {
  const ttlMs = configStore.get(`/tenants/${tenantId}/topics/${topic}/ttl`);
  const cutoff = Date.now() - ttlMs;
  for (const msg of storage.scan(tenantId, topic)) {
    if (msg.timestamp < cutoff) {
      storage.delete(msg.id);
      // emit tombstone to preserve replay semantics
      storage.appendTombstone({ tenantId, topic, id: msg.id });
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle TTL changes mid-flight without losing data consistency?
- How do you verify no live consumers are affected during purge windows?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical skills in implementing observability across Kafka-based event streaming pipelines—a critical real-world challenge as data flows through multiple topics and partitions.

## Key Concepts
- W3C Trace Context propagation across messaging systems
- Span continuity across producer/consumer boundaries
- Sampling strategy selection and trace baggage for correlation
- Observability during partition rebalance and backpressure scenarios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Inject trace context into outgoing message headers
function injectTraceContext(payload, tracer) {
  const span = tracer.startSpan('produce_order');
  const carrier = {};
  tracer.inject(span.context(), W3C_TRACE_CONTEXT, carrier);
  return { payload, headers: carrier };
}

// Extract trace context from incoming message
function extractTraceContext(message, tracer) {
  const spanContext = tracer.extract(W3C_TRACE_CONTEXT, message.headers);
  const span = tracer.startSpan('process_order', { childOf: spanContext });
  return span;
}

// Attach trace_id to enriched payload for cross-topic correlation
function enrichWithTraceId(payload, span) {
  return { ...payload, trace_id: span.spanContext().traceId };
}
```

## Key Considerations
- Use OpenTelemetry for vendor-agnostic instrumentation
- Ensure trace context propagation survives partition rebalances
- Consider tail-based sampling for cost-effective tracing of rare errors
- Monitor latency degradation during rebalance events

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes KCNA security design, tenant isolation, and practical key management choices under constraints like rotation and revocation.

## Key Concepts
- Envelope encryption with per-tenant DEKs
- Hierarchical KMS/HSM wrapping and rotation
- Per-tenant access checks and audit proofs
- Rollout/rollback with minimal downtime and observability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for envelope encryption
function encryptEvent(event, tenantId, kms, store) {
  const dek = store.getDEK(tenantId);
  const ciphertext = aesGcmEncrypt(event.payload, dek);
  return { ciphertext, headers: { tenantId, keyId: dek.id, epoch: dek.epoch } };
}
function decryptEvent(record, store) {
  const dek = store.getDEK(record.headers.tenantId, record.headers.keyId);
  return aesGcmDecrypt(record.ciphertext, dek);
}
```

